/**
 * @page search_optimization Search and coefficient optimization
 *
 * Search answers which candidates come next. It owns population state,
 * variation, selection, reinsertion, fitness caching, and Taskflow scheduling.
 * It orchestrates optional coefficient fitting before scoring an individual; it
 * does not define objective semantics or execution kernels.
 *
 * @section search-flow Generation lifecycle
 *
 * This UML activity view distinguishes actions (rounded rectangles), the
 * optional-fit decision (diamond), and the start/end nodes.
 *
 * @dot
 * digraph search {
 *   rankdir=LR;
 *   graph [bgcolor="transparent", pad=0.25, nodesep=0.45, ranksep=0.7];
 *   node [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10];
 *   edge [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9, color="#4a5568", fontcolor="#4a5568", arrowsize=0.7];
 *   start [shape=circle, label="", width=0.18];
 *   prepare [shape=box, style="rounded", label="Prepare selectors\nand evaluator"];
 *   variation [shape=box, style="rounded", label="Select / crossover / mutate"];
 *   fit [shape=diamond, label="Fit coefficients?"];
 *   optimize [shape=box, style="rounded", label="Coefficient fit"];
 *   score [shape=box, style="rounded", label="Score individual"];
 *   pool [shape=box, style="rounded", label="Offspring pool"];
 *   reinsert [shape=box, style="rounded", label="Reinsert\n(elites protected)"];
 *   next [shape=box, style="rounded", label="Next parents"];
 *   finish [shape=doublecircle, label="", width=0.18];
 *   start -> prepare -> variation -> fit;
 *   fit -> optimize [label="yes"];
 *   fit -> score [label="no"];
 *   optimize -> score;
 *   score -> pool -> reinsert -> next -> finish;
 * }
 * @enddot
 *
 * @section search-state Algorithm state and lifecycle
 *
 * `GeneticAlgorithmBase` owns one contiguous `individuals_` allocation. Its
 * `parents_` and `offspring_` spans are views into that allocation and must be
 * rebound after copy or restore; callers must not retain them beyond algorithm
 * mutation. It borrows `Problem`, initializers, generator, and reinserter.
 *
 * A generation prepares selectors and evaluator against the current parents,
 * produces candidates, evaluates and scores accepted candidates, then reinserts
 * from the pool. Evaluation budget termination belongs to the generator's
 * evaluator. `Reset()` is valid only between runs: it clears generation state,
 * timing, stop request, and evaluator counters.
 *
 * `OffspringGeneratorBase` owns no model state. It chooses two parents, applies
 * crossover and mutation probabilistically, then optionally runs a
 * `CoefficientOptimizer` before `ScoreIndividual`. Its optional Zobrist cache
 * maps a structural hash—not current coefficient values—to a fitness vector.
 * A cache miss is scored and inserted whether or not the `pLocal` trial ran.
 * Therefore, with `pLocal < 1`, the first encounter of a structure determines
 * the score later equivalent structures reuse until cache expiry or `Clear()`.
 * Enable this cache only when that structural-fitness policy is intended.
 *
 * `cache-max-age` is a lazy staleness limit, not a capacity limit: an old entry
 * is removed only when its hash is revisited. `0` disables expiry. Long-lived
 * embedding callers must call `Zobrist::Clear()` at their chosen run boundary
 * if they need to reclaim the table.
 *
 * @section search-orchestration Per-individual orchestration
 *
 * `OffspringGeneratorBase::Generate` constructs a child, then calls
 * `ScoreIndividual`. That function runs optional `LocalSearch`, scores the
 * resulting individual through the evaluator, and restores the original
 * coefficients when the Lamarckian trial did not inherit them. Coefficient
 * fitting therefore precedes scoring but remains outside the evaluator: its
 * optimizer uses execution residuals and Jacobians rather than objectives.
 *
 * @section search-parallelism Parallelism and caches
 *
 * GP and NSGA-II build Taskflow work in their algorithm runners. Evaluators,
 * interpreters, and optimizers instead receive per-worker scratch state; there
 * is no shared execution pipeline. The offspring generator owns the structural
 * fitness cache. Shape feasibility and JIT compilation keep separate caches in
 * their own subsystems, so a cache hit in one does not imply a hit in another.
 *
 *
 * `ReinserterBase` enforces elitism before each concrete combine strategy. The
 * protected prefix is ordered by the supplied comparison, so GP and NSGA-II
 * share the mechanism but supply different orderings. Keep-best merges parent
 * and pool candidates; replace-worst replaces the non-elite tail from a sorted
 * pool.
 *
 * @section search-algorithms GP and NSGA-II
 *
 * `GeneticProgrammingAlgorithm` is the single-objective runner. `NSGA2` adds a
 * nondominated sorter, Pareto fronts, and crowding distance; it exposes the
 * current best Pareto front through `Best()`. Objective count is fixed into
 * every individual fitness vector at NSGA-II construction. A comparator or
 * fitness layout mismatch is an algorithm configuration error, not something
 * reinsertion can repair.
 *
 * @section optimization Coefficient optimization
 *
 * `CoefficientOptimizer` wraps an `OptimizerBase` and returns a new tree plus
 * `FitOutcome`. It does not mutate the original candidate in place. The caller
 * decides whether successful fitted coefficients are inherited (Lamarckian) or
 * used only for the immediate score.
 *
 * `OptimizerBase::Optimize()` returns either `FitResult` or one of three
 * meaningful failures: a non-improving fit, an interpreter evaluation error,
 * or a configuration error such as invalid LM weights. Every outcome carries
 * `FitDiagnostics`, so reporting must use `Diagnostics()` rather than discard
 * counts on failure. Success means final cost improves on initial cost; a solver
 * finishing normally is not sufficient.
 *
 * Linear scaling is distinct from coefficient optimization. `FitLinearScaling`
 * fits `y = scale * f(x) + offset` against the training target, and may use a
 * caller-owned scratch output buffer. Its value and derivative interval
 * transforms differ because an offset vanishes under differentiation. Scaling
 * must remain consistent with the `Problem` policy and evaluator non-finite
 * handling.
 *
 * @section search-entrypoints API entry points
 *
 * - `operon/algorithms/ga_base.hpp`, `gp.hpp`, `nsga2.hpp`: algorithm state and runners.
 * - `operon/operators/generator.hpp`: candidate generation and cache boundary.
 * - `operon/operators/reinserter.hpp`: elitism and replacement policy.
 * - `operon/operators/local_search.hpp`: coefficient-optimization operator.
 * - `operon/optimizer/optimizer.hpp`: fit outcomes, diagnostics, LM/SGD implementations.
 * - `operon/operators/linear_scaling.hpp`: fitted model-output scaling.
 */
